{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"Split the outer conjunction, and for each `Iff` prove both sides independently—they are standard base-\(b\) facts, so no implication between the two sides is needed.

- For the first `Iff`, use fixed-length, little-endian base-\(b\) expansions. Let \(D_{b,l}\) be the lists of length \(l\) whose entries are \(<b\). Padding `digits` with trailing zeros gives `digitsAppend`, which is inverse to `ofDigits` between \(n<b^l\) and \(D_{b,l}\). Then identify \(D_{b,l}\) with `List.fixedLengthDigits hb l` and \(\{n\mid n<b^l\}\) with `Finset.range (b^l)`. This yields respectively `Nat.setInvOn_digitsAppend_ofDigits` and `Nat.bijOn_ofDigits'`.

- For the second `Iff`, the left side is the normalization fact that a nonzero number’s most significant digit is nonzero (`Nat.getLast_digit_ne_zero`). For the right side, transport the sum through the fixed-length digit bijection. Each of the \(l\) positions contains every digit \(0,\ldots,b-1\) exactly \(b^{l-1}\) times, so the total is
  \[
  l\,b^{\,l-1}\sum_{d<b}d
  =l\,b^{\,l-1}\binom b2.
  \]
  This is packaged as `Nat.sum_sum_digits_eq`.

The essential assumption \(1<b\) is what makes fixed-length base-\(b\) representations unique. These facts and their exact library statements are collected in [Mathlib.Data.Nat.Digits.Lemmas](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Data/Nat/Digits/Lemmas.html)."}}
